## 1. Introduction

Modern semiconductor architectures undergo countless layers of compiling, decoding, and scheduling to translate human programming languages into electrical signals that silicon transistors can understand. The resulting latency (Latency) and power dissipation have become critical bottlenecks to system performance improvement at a time when process scaling is hitting physical limits.

The NHE (Natural Hardware Embedding) $S\_s\_O$ architecture eliminates this translation layer entirely and introduces a direct-connection paradigm that aligns the software grammar directly with the hardware coordinate addresses—the "silicon-native language (Silicon-Native Language)." Because the instruction itself becomes the physical routing path (Routing Path) on the substrate, the execution speed converges to the speed of light ($\le 1\text{ Clock}$), and computational overflows and interferences ignored by compilers are fundamentally prevented at the substrate level.

This document specifies the logical and physical requirements of the NHE v2.0 architecture, which grants complete mathematical generalization and reconfigurability (Reconfigurability) so that the NHE philosophy can be applied to general-purpose hardware design.

