## 3. Polymorphic Soft-$S\_s\_O$: User-Defined Logical-to-Physical Mapping (Polymorphic Hardware-Defined $S\_s\_O$ Paradigm)

The uniqueness of the NHE $S\_s\_O$ programming language lies in the fact that physical addresses are directly equivalent to instruction syntax. However, this architecture does not subordinate this syntax to a specific fixed sensor or actuator. Through compiler configuration (Configuration Table) and virtual router settings of the FPGA/ASIC, users are delegated complete authority to define abstract roles for each component of $S$, $s$, and $O$.

```
+─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────+
|               User-Defined S_s_O Schema Table               |
+─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────+
|  Role  |  Neuromorphic Core   |      Crypto Engine   |  IoT Sensor  |
+--------+----------------------+----------------------+--------------+
|   S    | Synaptic Matrix (S)  | Key Register (S)     | Sensor ID (S)|
|   s    | Activation Level (s) | Rotate/XOR Mode (s)  | Threshold (s)|
|   O    | Dendrite Output (O)  | Cipher Stream (O)    | Transmit (O) |
+─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────+

```

To support this polymorphic mapping mechanism, the NHE control panel utilizes a Look-Up Register Array (LRA) inside the silicon substrate. Users can arbitrarily adjust the behavior of their custom $S$, $s$, and $O$ sets as follows:

* **Soft-Subject (Software-Defined Subject):** Virtualizes the register address space at the hardware level to arbitrarily assign memory addresses, external network interfaces, or triggers of other cores as the "Subject ($S$)."
* **Soft-Signal (Software-Defined Signal):** Flexibly defines not only fixed potential differences but also specific numerical addition values, timer counters, or dynamic conditional branches as the "Signal ($s$)."
* **Soft-Output (Software-Defined Output):** Freely maps not only physical pin discharge but also system bus buffers, virtual memory blocks, and interrupt queues for virtual machine acceleration as the "Output ($O$)."

