## 4. Scalable & Modular Architectural Topology: Planar to 3D Stacked IC (Scalable Modular Topology from Planar Chips to Multi-Layer Stacked ICs)

The NHE architecture is designed to simultaneously support single-die integration (Planar SoC Single-Chip) as well as complex multi-layer three-dimensional circuits (3D Stacked IC) and heterogeneous modular chiplet integrations (Heterogeneous Multi-Die/Chiplet).

```
       3D Stacked IC Mode Switching Routing Scheme
      
      [Layer 2: Secure/Crypto]  --->  M_d = 2 (N_S * N_s * N_O Range)
             ▲ (TSV Via)
      [Layer 1: AI / NPU]       --->  M_d = 1 (N_S * N_s * N_O Range)
             ▲ (TSV Via)
      [Layer 0: Spinal Reflex]  --->  M_d = 0 (N_S * N_s * N_O Range)

```

### [Formula 4.1] 4-Dimensional Signature Address Formula for Multi-Layer and Modular Expansion

By adding a layer (or chiplet unit module) dimension $M_d \in [0, M_{\text{max}}-1]$, we establish a physical address structure that prevents absolute address collisions even in multi-layer structures:


$$\mathcal{A}_{\text{Total}}(M_d, S_{idx}, s_{idx}, O_{idx}) = M_d \cdot (N_S \cdot N_s \cdot N_O) + S_{idx} \cdot (N_s \cdot N_O) + s_{idx} \cdot N_O + O_{idx}$$

### Engineering Merits:

* **Planar One-Chip (Single-Chip) Topology:**
By disabling $M_d = 0$ at the hardware level, resource overhead is minimized, and the system operates as a zero-latency control engine on a single planar die.
* **Multi-layer Stacked Structure (3D Stacked IC / TSV):**
The system immediately hops to upper layers through Through-Silicon Vias (TSVs). Address computation penetrates the multi-dimensional space in just 1-Clock cycle, without the intervention of bus arbiters or software schedulers during layer switching.
* **Heterogeneous Chiplet Integration (Heterogeneous Multi-Die/Chiplet Integration):**
At design time, users can randomly integrate third-party modular chips (e.g., motor control modules, RF wireless communication modules) designed with different specifications ($N_S, N_s, N_O$) onto the NHE bus.
Since each module possesses its own unique offset address range, hardware bus contention or electromagnetic signal crosstalk never occurs even under "Hot-Plugging" or random placement scenarios.

